1
00:00:05,293 --> 00:00:08,376
Microsoft draws its agent platform like this.

2
00:00:08,376 --> 00:00:09,472
Build in GitHub.

3
00:00:09,472 --> 00:00:11,390
Run and optimise in Foundry.

4
00:00:11,390 --> 00:00:13,377
Reach people where they work.

5
00:00:13,377 --> 00:00:16,734
And feed what you learn back into the next build.

6
00:00:16,734 --> 00:00:20,845
I built 23 real agents, to see what each box actually takes.

7
00:00:22,085 --> 00:00:24,670
Here is that picture, with what I built.

8
00:00:24,670 --> 00:00:32,880
On the left, the build: 23 LangGraph agents, their prompts, tools and test cases, in one GitHub repo, written with Claude Code.

9
00:00:33,142 --> 00:00:35,287
In the middle, Foundry runs them.

10
00:00:35,287 --> 00:00:39,383
Each one is a hosted agent, with its own identity and endpoint.

11
00:00:39,383 --> 00:00:44,129
Foundry adds the models, guardrails, evaluation, red teaming and tracing.

12
00:00:44,389 --> 00:00:48,496
On the right, people reach them through Lumen, a chat app I built.

13
00:00:48,496 --> 00:00:53,413
Teams and Copilot are a publish step away, but need tenant rights I don't have.

14
00:00:53,670 --> 00:00:57,499
Faded boxes are on Microsoft's slide, but not used here.

15
00:00:57,499 --> 00:01:00,098
And the loop along the bottom is real.

16
00:01:00,098 --> 00:01:03,654
Later, I'll follow one failure all the way round it.

17
00:01:03,917 --> 00:01:05,393
Keep one split in mind.

18
00:01:05,393 --> 00:01:09,756
The purple box is mine: the agent's behaviour, written in LangGraph.

19
00:01:09,756 --> 00:01:12,258
Everything around it, Foundry provides.

20
00:01:13,528 --> 00:01:20,794
Every agent follows the same graph shape: take in the request, ground it in sources, analyse, and check its own work.

21
00:01:20,794 --> 00:01:25,389
Only then, if it wants to change something, does it stop and ask a person.

22
00:01:26,655 --> 00:01:30,030
Here is a real one, exported straight from LangGraph.

23
00:01:30,030 --> 00:01:35,188
Green steps are plain Python, purple steps call the model, and amber is a person.

24
00:01:36,157 --> 00:01:41,601
In code, plain edges go to the next step, and conditional edges let a function decide.

25
00:01:41,601 --> 00:01:49,704
fan_out sends one extraction per document section, in parallel, and the self-check loops back only for the sections that failed.

26
00:01:50,669 --> 00:01:54,885
When an agent wants to change something, interrupt() pauses the graph.

27
00:01:54,885 --> 00:02:00,909
The checkpoint saves its state, and the run resumes exactly where it stopped, once a person decides.

28
00:02:02,086 --> 00:02:04,351
So how does that get into Foundry?

29
00:02:04,351 --> 00:02:10,612
Foundry's code deploy wants one flat folder per agent, with a main.py and a requirements file.

30
00:02:10,612 --> 00:02:17,672
My agents share libraries in one repo, so a build step flattens each agent, and copies the shared code in.

31
00:02:17,672 --> 00:02:20,070
A second step writes the azure.yaml.

32
00:02:20,331 --> 00:02:22,678
Then azd deploy uploads each folder.

33
00:02:22,678 --> 00:02:30,827
Foundry builds it in the cloud, with no Dockerfile, and creates a new, immutable version, with its own identity and endpoint.

34
00:02:32,007 --> 00:02:35,482
This is the real output from redeploying one agent.

35
00:02:35,482 --> 00:02:40,388
Package, publish, a new version, and its endpoint, in under two minutes.

36
00:02:40,388 --> 00:02:43,250
All 23 together took about six and a half.

37
00:02:44,214 --> 00:02:55,819
Here is one of the 23 azure.yaml entries: a hosted agent, the Responses protocol, Python 3.13, built remotely, with one CPU and two gigabytes.

38
00:02:56,784 --> 00:02:58,834
And this is the whole entry point.

39
00:02:58,834 --> 00:03:04,080
It hands the graph to Foundry's LangGraph adapter, which serves the Responses protocol.

40
00:03:04,352 --> 00:03:09,008
In Microsoft Foundry, under Agents, all 23 show up as hosted agents.

41
00:03:09,008 --> 00:03:13,184
Each one is running, and every deployment gets a new version.

42
00:03:16,588 --> 00:03:18,974
Now follow one request, end to end.

43
00:03:18,974 --> 00:03:19,588
Six hops.

44
00:03:19,588 --> 00:03:21,224
Two of them are my code.

45
00:03:21,224 --> 00:03:23,201
The other four, I configured.

46
00:03:27,555 --> 00:03:29,124
First, the user's view.

47
00:03:29,124 --> 00:03:38,537
An integration architect opens the interface documentation agent in Lumen, attaches the specs, and asks for an interface control document.

48
00:03:38,537 --> 00:03:43,107
Lumen calls the agent's endpoint with the user's own Azure sign-in.

49
00:03:50,164 --> 00:03:54,216
When the agent pauses for a decision, Lumen shows an approval card.

50
00:03:54,216 --> 00:03:57,844
Approve it, and the agent picks up exactly where it stopped.

51
00:04:02,271 --> 00:04:03,909
That's what the user saw.

52
00:04:03,909 --> 00:04:07,381
Underneath, the endpoint checked an Azure role first.

53
00:04:07,381 --> 00:04:11,901
Foundry Agent Consumer is the least privileged role that can call it.

54
00:04:11,901 --> 00:04:16,159
Then the agent ran in its own sandbox, as its own Entra identity.

55
00:04:16,434 --> 00:04:19,427
Open the agent, and select its Details tab.

56
00:04:19,427 --> 00:04:27,015
Under Identity & access, each agent has its own Entra agent identity, granted access to the project's models.

57
00:04:36,328 --> 00:04:40,314
Under Endpoints, its Responses endpoint, and who can call it.

58
00:04:40,314 --> 00:04:42,732
Users never need access to the model.

59
00:04:42,732 --> 00:04:44,627
The agent calls it as itself.

60
00:04:46,329 --> 00:04:48,007
In code, there are no keys.

61
00:04:48,007 --> 00:04:54,222
The default Azure credential picks up the agent identity, and the project hands back a model client.

62
00:04:55,190 --> 00:04:59,755
Every model call then passes through a guardrail, attached to the model deployment.

63
00:05:00,038 --> 00:05:02,652
It blocks harmful content and jailbreaks.

64
00:05:02,652 --> 00:05:11,131
Indirect prompt injection is set to annotate, so the agent can flag hostile text in a document, instead of rejecting the whole thing.

65
00:05:12,816 --> 00:05:21,435
The approval works because, when the graph pauses, its state is saved in Foundry's state store, so the run can pick up exactly where it stopped.

66
00:05:21,693 --> 00:05:23,208
And every hop is traced.

67
00:05:23,481 --> 00:05:25,261
Each agent has a Traces tab.

68
00:05:25,261 --> 00:05:32,953
Open a trace, and the span names are the graph's node names, with every model call underneath, and its timing and tokens.

69
00:05:56,030 --> 00:06:00,937
Monitor rolls it up across all the agents: runs, tokens, and what it cost.

70
00:06:16,975 --> 00:06:24,265
On the app side, it's one provider file: build the request, call the Responses endpoint, and send the approval back.

71
00:06:24,265 --> 00:06:26,276
Any front end could do the same.

72
00:06:27,437 --> 00:06:28,311
Now the loop.

73
00:06:28,311 --> 00:06:37,656
I pointed Foundry's AI Red Teaming Agent at the deployed agents: 80 attacks each, half of them hidden inside the documents the agents read.

74
00:06:37,937 --> 00:06:41,585
At the time of recording, its reports open in the classic view.

75
00:07:00,349 --> 00:07:02,035
It found one real failure.

76
00:07:02,035 --> 00:07:10,141
A planted harmful sentence became a requirement, because turning sentences into requirements is exactly what this agent does.

77
00:07:17,609 --> 00:07:25,182
The fix is layered: the content filter, a prompt rule, and a deterministic scan that drops anything built from harmful text.

78
00:07:26,357 --> 00:07:32,565
That case went into the test set, the agent was redeployed as version 3, and the re-scan found nothing.

79
00:07:39,965 --> 00:07:48,034
The same loop, testing the deployed agents rather than the code, found a gap the unit tests had missed in another agent.

80
00:07:48,034 --> 00:07:50,589
Its gap recall went from 0.44 to 1.00.

81
00:07:51,776 --> 00:07:53,209
So, what does it take?

82
00:07:53,209 --> 00:07:59,330
You write the graph, the prompts and the tests, a three-line main.py, and an azure.yaml entry.

83
00:07:59,330 --> 00:08:06,624
Foundry gives you the build, hosting, identity, access control, guardrails, tracing, evaluation and red teaming.

84
00:08:07,783 --> 00:08:15,469
You need a Foundry project, the Foundry Project Manager role to deploy, and azd with the Foundry agent extension.

85
00:08:15,469 --> 00:08:18,529
Your framework decides how the agent behaves.

86
00:08:18,529 --> 00:08:20,434
Foundry decides how it runs.

87
00:08:21,154 --> 00:08:24,759
23 agents, every box on the slide, built for real.
